title: eureka_context
---
// Eureka, the institute town of the Science dimension (gate address
// SCIENCE), on the ground under the orbital station: one node per resident,
// named `eureka_<resident>`, and the institute's own Patent Clerk and Carl
// Stargan. The space elevator goes up from the town's ground station to the
// station's tether hub.
//
// Everyday talk only (Jon, 2026-10-29: "we are world building not quest
// building"): no flags, no errands.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: eureka_teodora
---
Teodora: Car up at the hour, car down at the half. Step in, hold the rail, and do not lean on the door.
-> How far up does it go?
    Teodora: All the way to the station's tether hub. Thirty-six thousand kilometres of cable, and I have walked none of it.
    Teodora: The car does the climbing. I do the timetable. Both of us are on time.
-> What is up there?
    Teodora: Customs, a docking bay, people who eat out of tubes. Further along the spoke, Gnu-ton's gravity works.
    Teodora: The car comes back lighter some days. Nobody can tell me why. I log it anyway.
-> Do people take the gate instead?
    Teodora: The ring is quicker. It also leaves you sick on a bench for an hour. My car gives you a view.
-> Have a good shift.
===

title: eureka_priya
---
Priya: Is it day or night? Do not tell me. The run cannot know either.
-> How long have you been awake?
    Priya: Since the experiment started. It has been running four days. So have I.
    Priya: If I sleep, something drifts. If something drifts, I start again.
-> What are you measuring?
    Priya: Whether a clock at the top of the elevator runs fast. It does. The question is by how much, and whether my supervisor believes me.
-> You should rest.
    Priya: Mina said that. Then she sold me another coffee. I think she has a conflict of interest.
-> Good luck with it.
===

title: eureka_dmitri
---
Dmitri: Do not touch that. And do not touch that. You can touch that, it is already broken.
-> What do you fix?
    Dmitri: Everything that has a plug. Some things that do not. Last week a professor brought me a chalkboard that was "giving wrong answers".
-> What breaks most?
    Dmitri: The coil lab. Third fuse this week. They say the coils are fine. The fuses disagree.
-> What is the lever by the bench?
    Dmitri: The director's anti-gravity prototype. It works. That is the problem.
    Dmitri: If you press it, there is another one on the ceiling. I put it there after the first time.
-> Do you ever go up the elevator?
    Dmitri: Once a month, to fix the car's brakes. Up there I fix the coffee machine too. They pay me in thanks and dried soup.
-> I will leave you to it.
===

title: eureka_wilbur
---
Wilbur: Mind the grass. I keep it. It does not keep itself, whatever the physicists tell you.
-> Have you worked here long?
    Wilbur: Forty years on this quad. Three deans, two fires, one theory of everything. The grass outlasted all of them.
-> Do the other pigeons bother you?
    Wilbur: Do not start. They are not my relatives. They are not anyone's relatives. They eat crumbs off the lecture steps and look at me like I owe them.
-> What do you think of the elevator?
    Wilbur: A long rope to the sky. Lovely. Somebody has to sweep under it.
-> I will mind the grass.
===

title: eureka_halvorsen
---
Professor Halvorsen: You there. You look impartial. Read this paragraph and tell me it is clear.
-> What is it?
    Professor Halvorsen: My paper. Reviewer two says it is "unclear in places". He does not say which places. He never says which places.
-> Why are you in Eureka?
    Professor Halvorsen: I came through the gate to give one lecture. That was three months ago. I have been revising ever since.
    Professor Halvorsen: Math Town wanted me first. They said physics was "applied". I said they were "unemployed". We have not spoken.
-> Is reviewer two right?
    Professor Halvorsen: That is precisely the kind of question reviewer two would ask.
-> Good luck with the revisions.
===

title: eureka_mina
---
Mina: Coffee, soup, or a chair. The chair is free if you buy something.
-> Who comes here?
    Mina: Everybody on campus. Students in the morning, professors at noon, the lab crew when something has caught fire.
    Mina: Physics owes me eleven coffees. Chemistry pays in cash. Biology brings its own cups, which I respect.
-> What is the soup?
    Mina: Whatever came down on the elevator. Today it is lentils from the station. They sent it frozen and it arrived annoyed.
-> Do people from the station come down?
    Mina: On weekends. They sit by the window and stare at the sky like it might fall on them. It is lovely, honestly.
-> Just looking.
===

title: eureka_patent_clerk
---
Patent Clerk: Application received. Please take a number. Numbers are issued in the order they are observed.
-> What are you stamping?
    Patent Clerk: Claims. A perpetual motion machine, a faster elevator, and a hat that thinks. Two rejections and one request for a better drawing.
-> Is this where you work now?
    Patent Clerk: An office is an office. This one has a window on the cable. When I watch the car go up, my clock and the operator's disagree. I file that too.
-> What do you think of the elevator?
    Patent Clerk: It is a fine machine with a terrible timetable. Car up at the hour. Whose hour? Measured from where?
    Patent Clerk: Teodora says "the hour on the wall". I asked which wall. She has stopped answering my letters.
-> Carry on.
===

title: eureka_stargan
---
<<portrait_clip "wonder">>
Carl Stargan: Look up. No, further. That pale dot just above the cable is the station. Everyone you have met up there lives on that dot.
-> What do you study here?
    <<portrait_clip "explaining">>
    Carl Stargan: The sky. Billions and billions of stars, and on a clear night the elevator's cable cuts right through the middle of them.
-> Is the science dimension the only one with stars?
    <<portrait_clip "skeptical">>
    Carl Stargan: Math Town insists they are a separate dimension and their sky is "a set". I asked them to measure it. They proved it exists and went home.
-> Can I use the telescope?
    <<portrait_clip "explaining">>
    Carl Stargan: Of course. Look at the station first, then past it. It is good for you to see how small a big thing is.
-> Thank you.
===

title: eureka_pryce
---
Range Officer Pryce: Hard hat? No? Then stand behind me. Not that I am any harder.
-> What is tested here?
    Range Officer Pryce: Rockets, coils, a self-driving wheelbarrow. Whatever the institute builds, it comes out here to be found out.
    Range Officer Pryce: I sign the form. The form has two boxes: FINE and PROBABLY FINE. Guess which I tick.
-> Is the rocket safe?
    Range Officer Pryce: PROBABLY FINE III? It has a name with three in it. The first two taught us a great deal, briefly.
-> What are those dummies for?
    Range Officer Pryce: Crash tests. Hit one. Go on. They are paid in sandbags and they do not complain, which is more than I can say for the interns.
-> Stay safe.
    Range Officer Pryce: Probably.
===

title: eureka_hum
---
Ottoline Hum: (She holds one hand near the antenna, the other by the loop. A note wavers out of nothing, like a ghost clearing its throat.)
-> How do you play it without touching it?
    Ottoline Hum: Your hand and the antenna make a capacitor. Move closer, the pitch goes up. The other hand on the loop is the volume. You play the air between.
    Ottoline Hum: The trick is standing still. Breathe wrong and you're a semitone flat.
-> What are you playing?
    Ottoline Hum: Whatever the institute's working on. Monday it was the hydrogen spectrum. The physicists cried. The chemists asked for it in a minor key.
-> Do people pay?
    Ottoline Hum: Visitors off the gate pay in coins I've never seen. I've got a case of currencies from forty dimensions. The patent clerk says one of them is technically a button.
-> Play on.
    Ottoline Hum: Always. Mind you don't walk between me and the antenna. You'd be in the song.
===

title: eureka_quark
---
Dr. Ilse Quark: Visitor! Helmet's optional, curiosity isn't. Welcome to the Eureka Collider. This is the slow bit. The fast bit is in the pipe.
-> What does the collider do?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: We send two beams round the ring opposite ways and make them meet in the detector. Then we read the wreckage. It's the most expensive way anyone has ever found to break something to see what's in it.
-> Why the bicycles?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: The ring's twenty-seven kilometres round. The beam does it eleven thousand times a second. I do it once before lunch. We don't race. Well. I don't.
-> What has got loose in the tunnel?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: The range's crawlers come down the cable ducts for the warmth, and a shock warden followed them. Nobody's sure which way the shock warden's spin points. Mind it.
-> What is the quark in the chest?
    Dr. Ilse Quark: A spare. Charm flavour. You can't have a quark on its own, strictly, so don't open the box in front of a theorist.
===
